  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>A bag has 6 red and 4 blue balls.</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A bag has {@ball1@} {@ball1c@} and {@ball2@} {@ball2c@} balls. One ball is drawn, replaced, and then another is drawn. Find the probability that:</p>
<p></p>
<p>
     <ol type="a">
             <li><p>Both balls are {@ball2c@} [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p></li>
             <li><p>One ball is {@ball1c@} and the other is {@ball2c@} [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]][[feedback:prt3]]</p></li>
     </ol>

</p>

<p></p>
<p></p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><h3> Worked Solution</h3></p>
<p>The probability that:</p>
<p>
     <ol type="a">
             <li><p>Both balls are {@ball2c@}</p>
<p> To find the probability that both balls are {@ball2c@}, we multiply the probability of drawing a {@ball2c@} ball on the first turn by the probability of drawing one on the second turn.</p>
<p>Because the first ball is replaced before the second draw, the probability stays exactly the same for both draws:</p>
<p>{@pball2@} \(\times\) {@pball2@} = {@ta@}</p></li>
             <li><p>One ball is {@ball1c@} and the other is {@ball2c@}</p>
<p> To find the probability that one ball is {@ball1c@} and the other is {@ball2c@}, we calculate the probability of both possible ways and add them together:</p>
<p><strong>First way</strong> ({@ball1c@} then {@ball2c@}): Multiply {@pball1@} \(\times\) {@pball2@}</p>
<p>Second way ({@ball2c@} then {@ball1c@}): Multiply {@pball2@} \(\times\) {@pball1@}</p>
<p>Since both ways have the same probability, the final calculation is:2 \(\times\) ({@pball1@} \(\times\) {@pball2@}) = {@ta1@}</p></li>
     </ol>

</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
